'''James Edward Harden Jr.''' (born August 26, 1989) is an American professional [[basketball]] player for the [[Cleveland Cavaliers]] of the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA). He is widely regarded as one of the greatest [[shooting guard]]s and scorers in NBA history.<ref>{{cite web|first=John|last=Schuhmann|title=2015–16 GM Survey|url=http://www.nba.com/news/features/john_schuhmann/nba-com-2015-16-gm-survey/|website=NBA.com|date=October 20, 2015|access-date=June 28, 2017|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170306203301/http://www.nba.com/news/features/john_schuhmann/nba-com-2015-16-gm-survey/|archive-date=March 6, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|first=Frank|last=Blinebury|title=Harden stands out among pack of top shooting guards|url=http://www.nba.com/2015/news/features/fran_blinebury/10/12/top-10-shooting-guards-for-2015-16/|website=NBA.com|date=October 12, 2015|access-date=June 28, 2017|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170308221049/http://www.nba.com/2015/news/features/fran_blinebury/10/12/top-10-shooting-guards-for-2015-16/|archive-date=March 8, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|first=Andy|last=Bailey|title=NBA All-Time Player Rankings: Top 10 Shooting Guards|url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2852997-nba-all-time-player-rankings-top-10-shooting-guards|website=BleacherReport.com|date=September 13, 2019|access-date=December 10, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=20 greatest shooting guards ever: The HoopsHype list|url=https://hoopshype.com/lists/20-greatest-shooting-guards-ever-the-hoopshype-list/|website=HoopsHype.com|date=September 24, 2021|access-date=December 10, 2022|archive-date=November 7, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211107165051/https://hoopshype.com/lists/20-greatest-shooting-guards-ever-the-hoopshype-list/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Gus|last=Martin|title=Ranking The Top 10 Pure Scorers In NBA History|url=https://fadeawayworld.net/nba/ranking-the-top-10-pure-scorers-in-nba-history|newspaper=Fadeaway World|date=July 23, 2020|access-date=May 15, 2022}}</ref> In 2021, Harden was honored as one of the league's top 75 players by being named to the [[NBA 75th Anniversary Team]].<ref>{{cite web|title=NBA 75th Anniversary Team announced|url=https://www.nba.com/news/nba-75th-anniversary-team-announced|website=NBA.com|date=October 21, 2021|access-date=October 22, 2021}}</ref> Harden is also a two-time member of the [[United States men's national basketball team|United States national team]], winning gold medals at the [[Basketball at the 2012 Summer Olympics|2012 Summer Olympics]] and [[2014 FIBA Basketball World Cup|2014 FIBA World Cup]]. Harden is nicknamed "'''the Beard'''" after his characteristic facial hair.<ref>{{Cite web |title=What Is James Harden's Nickname? {{!}} {{!}} RevUp Sports |url=https://revupsports.com/athletes/basketball/james-harden/discover/what-is-james-hardens-nickname/#:~:text=James%20Harden,%20the%20NBA%20superstar,to%20his%20signature%20facial%20hair. |access-date=2025-05-16 |website=revupsports.com |language=en}}</ref>

Harden played [[college basketball]] for the [[Arizona State Sun Devils men's basketball|Arizona State Sun Devils]], where he was named a consensus [[NCAA Men's Basketball All-Americans|All-American]] and [[Pac-12 Conference Men's Basketball Player of the Year|Pac-10 Player of the Year]] in 2009. Harden was selected with the third overall pick in the [[2009 NBA draft]] by the [[Oklahoma City Thunder]]. In 2012, he was named [[NBA Sixth Man of the Year Award|NBA Sixth Man of the Year]] and helped Oklahoma City reach the [[2012 NBA Finals|NBA Finals]], where they lost to the [[2011–12 Miami Heat season|Miami Heat]] in five games. After the Thunder refused to offer him a max contract extension, Harden was unwilling to take a pay cut and was subsequently traded to the [[Houston Rockets]] before the 2012–13 season.

In his first season with the Rockets, Harden set or matched several team records and was named to his first [[All-NBA Team]] (a third-team selection), as well as his first [[NBA All-Star]] team. Over the next seven full seasons with Houston, Harden led the league in scoring three times and assists once, and was named the [[NBA Most Valuable Player]] in {{nbay|2017|end}} while leading the Rockets to the Western Conference Finals. He was also named to seven consecutive NBA All-Star teams and earned All-NBA First Team honors six times. After requesting a trade at the beginning of the 2020–21 season, Harden was traded to the [[Brooklyn Nets]] as part of a four-team trade. With Brooklyn, he was named to his ninth and tenth consecutive All-Star games before being traded to the [[Philadelphia 76ers]] at the 2022 trade deadline. In 2023, Harden led the league in assists for the second time in his career. He was traded to the [[Los Angeles Clippers]] at the start of the 2023–24 season and was named to his 11th All-Star Game in 2025.

